Portable limits in the C runtime template (bend2/comp.ts), unguarded; on
64-bit native targets the runtime behaves as before.

- Compile-time knobs with today's values as defaults, set with -D when
  compiling the emitted C: BEND_CORPUS_BYTES (the CPU corpus's first size,
  1ull << 33) and BEND_STACK_BYTES (each thread's machine stack, 1ull << 31).
  A 32-bit target (wasm32) cannot fit 8 GiB plus 2 GiB stacks in 4 GiB.
- size_fit: every u64 length passed to a size_t parameter (pool_try's mmap,
  corpus_map's and corpus_grow's munmap, io_out's fwrite, io_cbuf's
  malloc/realloc, io_wait's calloc/memset) fails with "bend: a size past
  the address space" when it exceeds the target's size_t, instead of
  truncating silently (on wasm32 the 8 GiB corpus became a 0-byte map and
  the runtime then wrote past it).
- corpus_map: when every address-hinted mmap fails, try once more with
  mmap(NULL, ...) before "reservation failed"; Emscripten's mmap refuses
  every hint with EINVAL. In-place growth (corpus_grow) is unchanged.

With this patch -Wshorten-64-to-32 reports no narrowing to size_t in the
emitted C. The remaining warnings are u64 to u32 narrowings (a32_fadd's
f32_rewrap, whose value is a 32-bit float's bits, and the emitter's own
u32 locals and task_node arguments); u32 is 32 bits on every target, so
clang reports the same ones on 64-bit native and they are not wasm32
truncations.

Retired by: an upstream runtime whose memory sizes are configurable and
checked against size_t, and whose corpus placement does not need an
address hint (https://github.com/bendlang/bend/issues/1598).

